---
/** About: people, how the archive was made, AI disclosure, privacy, corrections, lost tapes. */
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, PROJECT_CREDIT, SITES, siteUrl } from "@goldstein/config/sites";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { episodes, manifest, rows, SITE } from "../../lib/site-data.ts";

const m = manifest();
const pub = rows().filter((r) => r.preferred).length;
const lost = episodes().filter((e) => e.status === "lost").length;
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="About the Goldstein Story Archive: who made the recordings, how the archive was put together, privacy and corrections."
>
  <div class="wrap page about">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>About the archive</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {SITE.name} gathers the recorded stories of the Goldstein family: {pub.toLocaleString("en")}{" "}
        recordings, about {Math.round(m.hours)} hours, from the 1980s to today.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="reading">
      <h2>The people</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Rabbi Yosef Goldstein</strong>, known to a generation of children as{" "}
        <strong>Uncle Yossi</strong>, hosted
        <em>Tzivos Hashem on the Air</em>, a weekly radio program broadcast on Tuesday nights from
        late 1981 into the early 1990s. Each half-hour show opened with children saying Shema at the
        microphone, answered letters from the mailbox, taught about the coming Yom Tov and the
        mitzvah campaigns, and carried a serial story from week to week. He also gave the Yiddish
        Tanya shiurim in the archive and hosted the earlier program <em>Shema B'ni</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        His son <strong>Rabbi Chaim Levi Goldstein</strong> tells most of the stories outside the
        radio show, and is known for the song “I Believe in Hashem”, which opens his stories.{" "}
        <strong>Bubby Chaya Malka</strong>, Uncle Yossi's mother, tells stories of her own in
        English and Yiddish.
      </p>

      <h2>How the archive was made</h2>
      <p>
        The tapes and files were collected, digitised and transcribed by machine. An AI model then
        listened through the transcripts and wrote the summaries, lessons, chapter marks, dates,
        tags and links between recordings, and a person checked the decisions that matter.{" "}
        <strong>Transcripts are automatic and summaries are written with AI</strong>: both may
        contain errors. The audio itself is never edited; where a tape ends in blank space the
        player simply stops at the end of the content.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yiddish recordings have no readable transcript on the site. Their words can still be found
        through search, so a Yiddish shiur on a given perek or a story told in Yiddish is not lost
        to a searcher.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        On the radio show, children came to the studio to say Shema, and birthdays, addresses and
        lists of people needing a refuah were read on air. The recordings are published as they were
        broadcast, but the site never shows those names, addresses or lists: those parts of the
        transcripts are withheld (“not transcribed”), and the chapters where they occur carry only a
        generic title. If you find personal details that should not be here, please write to us.
      </p>

      <h2>Lost tapes</h2>
      <p>
        {lost} episodes of the radio show are not in the archive yet.{" "}
        <a href="/radio/lost/">See the list</a>. If you recorded the program at home, or have any of
        these tapes, we would be very glad to hear from you.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections and contact</h2>
      <p>
        Every recording page has a “Report an error in this record” link that fills in an email for
        you. For anything else, write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Related sites</h2>
      <p>
        For children: <a href={siteUrl("kids")}>{SITES.kids.name}</a>. At bedtime:{" "}
        <a href={siteUrl("bedtime")}>{SITES.bedtime.name}</a>, whose nightly playlists also appear
        here under <a href="/tonight/">Tonight's playlists</a>. The data behind all three is{" "}
        <a href="/data/">available for download</a>.
      </p>
      <p>{PROJECT_CREDIT}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about h2 {
    margin-top: 1.6em;
    font-size: 21px;
  }
</style>
